mfd: twl-core: keep the HFCLKIN reference clock enabled

clocks_init() only looked up the "fck" clock to read its rate and dropped it
again, so nothing holds a reference on the PMIC's HFCLKIN source. On the Nest
J49 (OMAP3630 + TPS65921) that source is sys_clkout1; clk_disable_unused()
gates it at late_initcall and the PMIC disappears from I2C1 (NACK, arbitration
lost, SCL stuck low), taking USB VBUS detection, the regulators and the RTC with
it. Take the clock with devm_clk_get_enabled() instead so a DT
'clocks = <&sys_clkout1>; clock-names = "fck"' on the twl node keeps it alive.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: J49 project <j49@users.noreply.github.com>

diff -Nur a/drivers/mfd/twl-core.c b/drivers/mfd/twl-core.c
--- a/drivers/mfd/twl-core.c	2026-10-31 16:38:35.711870710 +0100
+++ b/drivers/mfd/twl-core.c	2026-10-31 16:38:35.756143746 +0100
@@ -629,7 +629,13 @@
 	u32 rate;
 	u8 ctrl = HFCLK_FREQ_26_MHZ;
 
-	osc = clk_get(dev, "fck");
+	/*
+	 * Keep the HFCLKIN reference running for as long as the PMIC is bound:
+	 * on boards where it comes from an SoC clock output (e.g. OMAP3
+	 * sys_clkout1) clk_disable_unused() would otherwise gate it and take
+	 * the PMIC off the I2C bus (NACKs, SCL stuck low, lost regulators).
+	 */
+	osc = devm_clk_get_enabled(dev, "fck");
 	if (IS_ERR(osc)) {
 		printk(KERN_WARNING "Skipping twl internal clock init and "
 				"using bootloader value (unknown osc rate)\n");
@@ -637,7 +643,6 @@
 	}
 
 	rate = clk_get_rate(osc);
-	clk_put(osc);
 
 	switch (rate) {
 	case 19200000:
